WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.440
If you want to know the truth if Hoffman could
have survived at the end of the Saw movies...

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.867
then stick around to the end of this video.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:15.250
Throughout Jigsaw’s active years, he brought
on a few disciples to help him carry out his

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:17.789
plans, and eventually take over for him after
his passing.

00:00:17.789 --> 00:00:21.800
One of these students would go on to overshadow
John Kramer himself, and that was Lieutenant

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:23.250
Detective Mark Hoffman.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:27.200
Welcome to Horror History, my name is Admiral
Chancellor Professor CZsWorld, and in today’s

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.970
lesson we’re going over the entire known
history of the most evil man to bear the Jigsaw

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.267
name so far, Mark Hoffman.

00:00:33.267 --> 00:00:37.260
Hoffman may have been partially named after
a well-known American criminal, Mark William

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.710
Hoffmann, who similarly spun into a murderous
episode after his plans unravel.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:45.200
His name was also a tribute to the late Gregg
Hoffman, one of the trio of producers responsible

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.440
for Saw 1 and 2, who is still honored in the
credits of every movie.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.520
The Saw character Hoffman did not start as
a psychopath though, he never even had a criminal

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:56.149
record during his lifetime, so to understand
his journey, let’s go back to his earliest

00:00:56.149 --> 00:00:57.887
mention in the series.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:04.949
Hoffman was likely born in the mid 60s.

00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:07.170
In 1976, his sister Angelina was born.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.799
It is not known what happened to his parents,
but in his adulthood, Angelina was his only

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:11.799
family member.

00:01:11.799 --> 00:01:13.979
“I know she was your only family.”

00:01:13.979 --> 00:01:18.000
After graduating from Renfield University,
Mark Hoffman became a cop in the early 80s.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.639
“I’ve been a cop for 20 years. Is that long enough for you?”

00:01:21.639 --> 00:01:25.399
One of his earliest known cases took place
at an elementary school, where he and Officer

00:01:25.399 --> 00:01:27.490
Daniel Rigg investigated an abusive teacher.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:31.359
They are unable to get solid evidence against
the guy, and a frustrated Rigg overreacts

00:01:31.359 --> 00:01:34.415
to a slap on the back and punches the teacher
in the face.

00:01:36.595 --> 00:01:38.119
"IN THE FACE!!"

00:01:38.119 --> 00:01:41.919
Hoffman claims that the teacher attacked Rigg
first, which is a long stretch of the truth,

00:01:41.919 --> 00:01:44.279
and signs an affidavit vowing that he witnessed
it.

00:01:44.279 --> 00:01:48.380
I see this as the first example of Hoffman’s
belief that it is OK to sidestep required

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:52.219
procedures to bring someone to justice, or
at least what he views as justice.

00:01:52.219 --> 00:01:55.590
In this case, he believes the teacher is guilty,
and he bends the truth in order to see that

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:56.590
he is locked up.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:59.630
He would later use a similar tactic to try
to bring another criminal to justice, but

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:01.289
this time, it would be personal.

00:02:01.289 --> 00:02:05.539
In 1997, Hoffman’s sister, Angelina, was
killed during a domestic dispute with her

00:02:05.539 --> 00:02:06.539
boyfriend, Seth Baxter.

00:02:06.539 --> 00:02:08.789
She was just 25 years old.

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:11.250
Mark felt helpless after losing his only family.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:15.209
His job was to serve and protect, but he was
unable to protect the only person that mattered

00:02:15.209 --> 00:02:16.209
to him.

00:02:16.209 --> 00:02:18.569
He became an alcoholic and lost all joy in
his life.

00:02:18.569 --> 00:02:22.680
This gets the attention of John Kramer, whose
goal is to make sure everyone cherishes their

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:23.680
lives.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.120
“You sit in bars until closing.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.220
You drink so you can sleep.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:32.230
You stagger to your car and then you start
in all over again the next day.”

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.450
But John doesn’t take action on Hoffman
quite yet.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:38.870
After a long trial, Baxter is eventually convicted
and sentenced to 25 years in prison starting

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:39.870
in 1999.

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:43.799
In the meantime, there is another police incident
that tells us more about Hoffman’s character.

00:02:43.799 --> 00:02:47.890
While responding to a distress call at a flophouse
full of junkies, he saves the life of another

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:49.659
officer named Matt Gibson.

00:02:49.659 --> 00:02:53.569
After ordering the junkie to drop the weapon,
Hoffman proceeds to shoot him several times.

00:02:53.569 --> 00:02:57.000
Like the incident at the school, Hoffman is
taking the law into his own hands rather than

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.690
letting due process play out.

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:00.640
He also sees this as a favor to Gibson…

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:01.760
“You owe me.”

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:05.689
...but Gibson, one of the few cops who is
not corrupt, reports him for brutality.

00:03:05.689 --> 00:03:06.689
“What brutality?”

00:03:06.689 --> 00:03:10.150
However, he still saved an Officer’s life,
so Hoffman ends up getting promoted.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:13.680
One year later, Gibson transferred to Internal
Affairs, where he busted three of Hoffman’s

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.180
men, and Hoffman swore he’d have his revenge.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.079
Just remember that, because it could come
back.

00:03:18.079 --> 00:03:19.829
I’m not saying it’s gonna come back……..

00:03:19.829 --> 00:03:21.110
But it might come back.

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:25.090
But for now, let’s fast forward a couple
more years to May of 2004.

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:28.709
Seth Baxter had served 5 years for the murder
of Hoffman’s sister, at which point he was

00:03:28.709 --> 00:03:30.280
released on a technicality.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:31.870
Seems to happen a lot in this city.

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:35.890
At this point, the police department had recently
formed a serial killer task force responsible

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:39.521
for solving a series of murders all accredited
to a criminal who was beginning to become

00:03:39.521 --> 00:03:41.459
known as the Jigsaw killer.

00:03:41.459 --> 00:03:45.480
Hoffman was not an investigator on the first
couple cases, but is aware of Jigsaw’s activity,

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.340
which gives him an idea of how he could get
revenge on Seth Baxter.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:52.709
That June, he creates a trap similar in style
to the traps that Jigsaw had used to kill

00:03:52.709 --> 00:03:54.670
his victims, but with one key difference.

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:58.709
Hoffman’s trap, which involved a giant swinging
pendulum, was inescapable.

00:03:58.709 --> 00:04:02.450
Hoffman took pleasure in watching his sister’s
killer get torn in half through a peephole

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:05.849
in the wall, but little did he know, Jigsaw
was still keeping an eye on him, and knew

00:04:05.849 --> 00:04:07.650
about his copycat crime.

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:11.330
Jigsaw waited in the elevator at Hoffman’s
apartment complex to sedate him and drag him

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:12.330
to his workshop.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:15.599
When he awakes, he finds himself in a real
Jigsaw trap, where his arms are tied to a

00:04:15.599 --> 00:04:19.220
chair and wired to a shotgun, so that too
much movement would pull the trigger.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:22.810
John explains that he believes everyone deserves
a second chance, and that in his games, if

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:25.460
the player survives, they are instantly rehabilitated.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:28.880
John did not appreciate Hoffman ripping off
his work, because Hoffman’s game did not

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:30.920
offer the victim a chance to survive.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:33.600
But true to his word, John is going to give
Hoffman a chance.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.720
He lets him out of the chair, and tells him
that he can try to arrest or kill him, but

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:40.020
if he does so, the truth about Hoffman’s
guilt will automatically be released.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:43.930
So Hoffman is given no choice but to join
Jigsaw in his work to test and rehabilitate

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:45.880
others who no longer cherished their lives.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:49.590
By joining him, Hoffman had passed Jigsaw’s
first test, and would begin learning his moral

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:51.590
code and methods of rehabilitation.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:55.270
During the construction of the next Jigsaw
trap, John would impart one of the most important

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:58.880
rules onto his pupil.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.180
The first victim that Hoffman assisted Jigsaw
in capturing was a man named Paul whose will

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.490
to live would be tested in a maze of barbed
wires.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:14.830
During the setup of this trap, Jigsaw explains
one of the most important rules of the operation.

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:15.830
Roll it.

00:05:16.886 --> 00:05:19.366
“The heart cannot be involved.

00:05:19.366 --> 00:05:21.789
Emotionally, there can be nothing there.

00:05:21.789 --> 00:05:24.699
It can never be personal.”

00:05:24.699 --> 00:05:29.419
Which seems kind of hypocritical because almost
everyone John tested was connected to him

00:05:29.419 --> 00:05:32.889
personally… but maybe this is a rule just
for Hoffman, because he already went after

00:05:32.889 --> 00:05:36.069
his sister’s ex-boyfriend with the pendulum
trap, and Jigsaw feared he would use the platform

00:05:36.069 --> 00:05:37.860
for personal reasons again.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:41.430
Using his inside knowledge as a metropolitan
police detective, Hoffman warns John that

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:44.430
there is a cop who is onto him, a man by the
name of David Tapp.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:48.100
John plans to throw Tapp off by having Hoffman
plant Dr. Lawrence Gordon’s penlight at

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:49.160
one of the crime scenes.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:52.770
He does this at the site of the next trap,
where a victim is burned alive trying to solve

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:54.240
Jigsaw and Hoffman’s puzzle.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.379
As far as we know, Hoffman was not involved
in the other traps that took place in 2004,

00:05:58.379 --> 00:06:02.520
because Jigsaw wanted to recruit new followers,
like the previously mentioned Lawrence Gordon

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.080
and a junkie named Amanda Young.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.860
Amanda’s second test took place in October
of 2005, when a group of criminals would be

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:10.419
forced to take part in a game at The Nerve
Gas House.

00:06:10.419 --> 00:06:14.139
This was the most complex and riskiest game
Jigsaw had ever held, so he enlisted the help

00:06:14.139 --> 00:06:15.849
of Hoffman to set it up and test it.

00:06:15.849 --> 00:06:19.300
It is here that Hoffman learns never to assume
any outcome.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:24.629
“I anticipate the possibilities and let
the game play out.”

00:06:24.629 --> 00:06:26.340
“Then why do you need Amanda in the game?”

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:28.250
“To ensure that the rules are followed.”

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:31.630
Hoffman seems to think that this method leaves
too much to chance, once again highlighting

00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:34.560
his belief that he should be a judge rather
than just an enforcer.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.659
It’s becoming apparent that this is his
biggest flaw, the reason that he’ll never

00:06:37.659 --> 00:06:40.340
fully realize the Jigsaw moniker in the future.

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:43.770
John is wary of this, and begins to put a
number of safeguards in place so that Hoffman

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:46.550
isn’t just given free reign in the event
of his passing.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:50.460
But John does survive the events of the Nerve
Gas House Game in 2005, and goes to work on

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:54.389
his next two games which would take place
simultaneously in April of 2006.

00:06:54.389 --> 00:06:58.439
In the meantime, Hoffman still has to keep
up with his actual job as a police detective.

00:06:58.439 --> 00:07:01.669
He investigates the classroom trap.... (looks
around)

00:07:01.669 --> 00:07:06.169
...the classroom trap, which was created by
another Jigsaw apprentice, Amanda Young.

00:07:06.169 --> 00:07:09.990
He has to kind of play dumb at the crime scene,
because he obviously knew that this one wasn’t

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:13.030
created by Jigsaw himself, but he wasn’t
about to make himself look guilty by being

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:14.120
the one to point it out.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:18.180
Though he could have used reverse psychology
to make himself look less guilty by actively

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:19.250
pointing it out.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:23.350
But maybe he was using reverse-reverse-psychology,
knowing that the other cops would expect a

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:25.270
guilty person to try using reverse psychology.

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:26.730
But then again…

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:29.650
🤘 Metal Music 🎸

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:32.879
Sorry, that’s literally what my brain is
like… all the time.

00:07:32.879 --> 00:07:36.099
In order to keep up the appearance that he’s
actively looking for Jigsaw, he brings in

00:07:36.099 --> 00:07:38.759
Jill Tuck, the ex-wife of John Kramer, for
interrogations.

00:07:38.759 --> 00:07:42.800
He tries to make her look guilty by asking
why she is in possession of a doll resembling

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:46.690
Billy the Puppet, which is the avatar used
in many of the videotapes Jigsaw left for

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:47.690
his victims.

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:50.910
He also presses her about having the tricycle
that Billy is often seen riding, which she

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:52.960
claims is a relic from John’s youth.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:55.900
Supposedly there are hours and hours of interrogation
footage.

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:59.919
The next victim to be tested is one of Hoffman’s
fellow investigators, a woman named Alison

00:07:59.919 --> 00:08:00.919
Kerry.

00:08:00.919 --> 00:08:04.009
Jigsaw wanted to test her because he believed
she was dead on the inside.

00:08:04.009 --> 00:08:07.219
Amanda was responsible for setting up this
trap as well, and Hoffman lended her a hand

00:08:07.219 --> 00:08:09.610
in hoisting the unconscious body into the
trap.

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:13.390
This would ultimately be a mistake on Hoffman’s
part, one that would lead the FBI to determine

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:16.740
that there were other Jigsaw apprentices besides
Amanda, because she would not have been strong

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:19.199
enough to lift Detective Kerry into the trap
on her own.

00:08:19.199 --> 00:08:22.710
Hoffman has to play dumb again at the crime
scene where Kerry is discovered, causing the

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:27.039
two FBI Agents, Peter Strahm and Lindsey Perez
to become somewhat suspicious of him.

00:08:27.039 --> 00:08:30.130
Over time, Strahm would become Hoffman's biggest
obstacle.

00:08:30.130 --> 00:08:33.010
Hoffman makes some last minute adjustments
on the day that two games would be played

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:35.780
simultaneously on April 28th, 2006.

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:39.640
One involves a man named Jeff Denlon who must
face a series of trials to forgive the people

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:42.200
involved in a drunk driving accident that
killed his son.

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:45.220
Hoffman was responsible for sedating and bringing
in the drunk driver.

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:48.890
John was critical when he saw the way Hoffman
treated the body of the man being tested.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:52.630
John also offers him some engineering pointers
to better optimize the machine, which is known

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:54.920
as The Rack, that the drunk driver would be
restrained in.

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:57.610
This is an interesting interaction for two
reasons.

00:08:57.610 --> 00:09:01.410
Hoffman’s carelessness with the victim shows
us his subconscious desire to do harm.

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:05.890
His goal for taking part in Jigsaw’s operations
is more about punishing wrongdoers than rehabilitating

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:06.890
them.

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:08.650
That’s not something that John Kramer would
approve of.

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:12.260
But the second part of the interaction shows
that Hoffman isn’t just there to assist

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:16.190
Jigsaw with the creation of his traps -- he’s
actively learning about mechanical engineering

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:19.460
because he will be expected to create traps
of his own in the future.

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:23.170
This means that Hoffman is gaining the trust
of Jigsaw, and he may one day be allowed to

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:26.430
take on the Jigsaw name in order to continue
John’s work.

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:30.320
However, Amanda was receiving similar training,
and if Hoffman wanted full control to punish

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:33.990
whoever he pleases, he would have to get her
out of the picture, so the two develop a bit

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:34.990
of a rivalry.

00:09:35.858 --> 00:09:38.850
AMANDA: “Get used to me, I’m not going anywhere.”

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:40.930
HOFFMAN: “You sure about that?”

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:41.930
MARIO: "No."

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:45.720
At first, it seems that Hoffman is just taunting
Amanda, but it soon becomes clear that he

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:49.060
has a plan to get rid of her, a plan he was
even able to sneak underneath the nose of

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:50.560
Jigsaw himself.

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:54.160
Outside of Jeff Denlon, there are several
others being tested on April 28th.

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:56.630
One of these is Jeff’s wife, a surgeon named
Lynn Denlon.

00:09:56.630 --> 00:10:00.490
Her job is to keep a dying John Kramer alive
as long as possible to make her appreciate

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:01.570
life again.

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:05.360
Amanda is also being tested, because Jigsaw
found out she was letting her emotions interfere

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:08.990
with the natural result of each game by putting
people that she felt should be punished through

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:12.450
inescapable traps, not too different from
what Hoffman did with the Pendulum.

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:16.210
So Amanda's role is to keep Lynn Denlon alive,
so Lynn can keep John Kramer alive.

00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:19.801
Amanda doesn’t know she’s being tested,
but Hoffman does, and he comes up with a plan

00:10:19.801 --> 00:10:23.600
to use this knowledge to his advantage in
an attempt to get rid of Amanda and Jigsaw

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:30.390
in one fell swoop, leaving him as the last
remaining man in the operation.

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:39.760
With the deaths of Sing, Tapp and Kerry and
with the disappearance of Eric Matthews, Hoffman

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:42.950
was one of the last remaining detectives on
the serial killer task force.

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:46.960
He was there to investigate both traps that
Amanda rigged to be inescapable, so he knew

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:51.140
better than anyone that her weakness was her
fiery emotion, and figured out a way to use

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:52.140
that against her.

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:56.140
While John is on his deathbed, he asks Hoffman
to place a note for Amanda to find, and he

00:10:56.140 --> 00:10:58.100
changes it out with a message of his own....

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:01.420
But before she would open it, Hoffman had
some other business to attend to.

00:11:01.420 --> 00:11:03.960
He first reports back Jigsaw in secret.

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:07.650
This would be his last face to face with him,
since John didn’t have much longer to live.

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:11.940
John’s last request to Hoffman is to carry
out another game on his behalf after his death.

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:15.710
He hands him an envelope with the instructions,
and Hoffman tucks it away for later.

00:11:15.710 --> 00:11:18.690
First, he had another set of tasks to take
care of.

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:22.140
Remember how there was supposed to be a simultaneous
game being played on April 28th?

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:24.600
Well, Hoffman had a lot of work to do to get
that one started.

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:28.080
He has to kidnap Rigg and place him at the
start of his trial, retrieve another cop,

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:31.670
Eric Matthews, and put him on a block of ice,
and presumably also subdue a lawyer named

00:11:31.670 --> 00:11:35.860
Art Blank, put him in this neck trap thingy,
and leave him Jigsaw’s instructions on what

00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:37.320
to do if he wants to survive.

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:41.450
And then I guess has to kidnap himself, and
go to the room where this is all taking place.

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:44.310
I know that sounds a bit, confusing because…
it is.

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:46.630
But let’s break down how everything is connected.

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:50.850
The main one being tested here is Rigg, because
Jigsaw has determined he’s become too obsessed

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:54.550
with saving his comrades, and isn’t living
his life or spending time with his wife anymore.

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:57.550
There are probably worse people out there
that could be tested, but that's who John

00:11:57.550 --> 00:12:00.330
chose, and as long as John is around, Hoffman
has to obey his orders.

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:04.420
In order for Rigg to pass, he simply has to
NOT try to save his co-workers Matthews and

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:05.420
Hoffman.

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:08.490
Matthews is being tested because he’s been
a perpetrator of police brutality.

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:11.950
He already failed Jigsaw’s previous test,
which you can learn about in this video, but

00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:15.680
his new test is to see whether or not he’ll
use excessive force to try to escape.

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:18.540
Art Blank is a lawyer being tested for defending
guilty people.

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:21.860
You may remember all those years ago when
Hoffman and Rigg had the run in with the abusive

00:12:21.860 --> 00:12:22.860
teacher?

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:24.210
Well, this is the guy who represented him.

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:28.200
Hoffman’s role in all of this is to be the
spectator, and make sure the rules are followed.

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:32.000
Just as John Kramer pretended to be dead in
the original Saw, Amanda pretended to be part

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:35.470
of the game in Saw 2, Hoffman wanted to keep
an eye on this game as well.

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:39.667
DET. KERRY: “Looks like our friend Jigsaw likes to book
himself front row seats to his own sick little games."

00:12:39.667 --> 00:12:43.020
It appeared that if Matthews failed to stay
on the ice block, the water would come down

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:45.300
and electrocute Hoffman as well… but, no.

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:48.490
He wasn’t actually in danger because he
had no reason to put himself in danger when

00:12:48.490 --> 00:12:49.490
he set up the game.

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:51.280
It was also just to motivate Eric Matthews.

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:55.240
So long story short though, Rigg and Matthews
both fail their tests, so Hoffman is the only

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:56.480
one to walk away alive.

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:59.500
But while that’s all happening, let’s
not forget that that other game is still going

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:00.630
on at the same time.

00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:04.170
The one with Lynn Denlon, Jeff Denlon, Amanda
Young and Jigsaw.

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:06.070
Remember how Hoffman left that note for Amanda?

00:13:06.070 --> 00:13:09.890
The note that was his ultimate move to eliminate
Amanda and Jigsaw, who were standing in his

00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:11.220
way of complete freedom?

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:12.220
Yes, that note?

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:13.940
How about we roll it?

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:16.940
“Dear pesky plumbers.”

00:13:16.940 --> 00:13:17.950
Aggh!

00:13:17.950 --> 00:13:19.000
Wrong note!

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:20.030
Roll the other note.

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:24.770
“Amanda - You were with Cecil the night
Jill lost Gideon.

00:13:24.770 --> 00:13:26.670
You killed their child.

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:32.840
You know it and I know it, so do exactly as
I say.

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:38.060
Kill Lynn Denlon or I will tell John what
you did.”

00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:41.360
Hoffman is referring to the night that John
Kramer's unborn child was killed by an addict

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:42.360
named Cecil.

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:46.500
John does not know that Amanda was in on it
too, and Amanda, for obvious reasons, doesn't

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:47.870
really want him to find out.

00:13:47.870 --> 00:13:51.320
If you're wondering how Hoffman even knows,
I'm guessing it's because he's a cop and probably

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:54.500
reviewed security footage after the break
in was reported, but they didn't really have

00:13:54.500 --> 00:13:58.520
any evidence that Amanda was part of it, and
John never pressed charges on Cecil anyway

00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:01.300
so at the end of the day the cops knew, but
John didn't.

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:05.070
So Amanda caves into Hoffman's threat, and
kills Lynn Denlon at the end of the game.

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:07.920
That causes Jeff Denlon to want to kill Amanda,
which he does.

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:11.760
Then, Jeff Denlon, who is still in a rage,
kills Jigsaw for putting him and his family

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:12.760
through all of this.

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:14.600
This is a huge deal for Hoffman.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:18.190
Remember, the only reason he began helping
Jigsaw in the first place was because Jigsaw

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:21.510
threatened to release the truth about Hoffman
getting revenge on his sister’s killer if

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:22.510
he didn’t comply.

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:27.080
That’s the main reason he helped Jigsaw
from the events of the first Saw in 2004 through

00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:28.530
Saw 4 in 2006.

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:32.600
But now, with Jigsaw and Amanda both out of
the picture, Hoffman’s secret was safe.

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:35.240
There was nothing forcing him to continue
Jigsaw’s work anymore.

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:38.070
He could easily go back to being a regular
cop at this point.

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:41.140
But this is the most interesting part of Hoffman’s
character arc.

00:14:41.140 --> 00:14:43.390
Some part of him liked being the hand of Jigsaw.

00:14:43.390 --> 00:14:47.730
I think it goes back to the reason he got
involved with all of this: his sister Angelina.

00:14:47.730 --> 00:14:51.440
Hoffman must have felt in some way, that by
bringing her killer to justice, he was entitled

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:54.240
to judge the fates of any future criminals
in the city.

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:57.310
He had become a cop to protect people, but
found that the police didn’t have enough

00:14:57.310 --> 00:14:59.990
power to enforce the law in a way that he
sees fit.

00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:01.690
That’s why he started bending the rules.

00:15:01.690 --> 00:15:04.800
It started when he signed the affidavit for
Rigg to get the abusive teacher thrown in

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:05.800
jail.

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:07.860
It continued when he murdered the junkie who
threatened Matt Gibson.

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:12.070
And it reached its peak when Hoffman literally
became Jigsaw, who was now a feared symbol

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:14.300
of twisted vigilante justice.

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:18.280
In fact, over the last two years, as Hoffman
got closer and closer to taking Amanda Young

00:15:18.280 --> 00:15:22.830
and John Kramer out of the equation, he developed
a condition known as megalomania.

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:26.700
Some of you may be more familiar with the
greek version of the word: megalovania.

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:29.080
🎵 Megalovania - Undertale (parody) 🎵

00:15:29.080 --> 00:15:32.950
Today, megalomania is known by mental health
professionals as Narcissistic Personality

00:15:32.950 --> 00:15:36.430
Disorder, but we’re still gonna be calling
it megalomania, because it’s way, way more

00:15:36.430 --> 00:15:37.430
fun to say.

00:15:37.430 --> 00:15:41.240
I think I’m actually gonna say megalomania
as much as possible from now on.

00:15:41.240 --> 00:15:44.500
Megalomania is characterized by an intense
obsession with power, and delusions that they

00:15:44.500 --> 00:15:46.800
are more important than anyone around them.

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:51.350
Other symptoms include calculative or manipulative
behavior, violent tendencies, egotism, lack

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:54.420
of empathy, entitlement and a God complex.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:55.850
Cock it and pull it.

00:15:55.850 --> 00:15:59.760
With his new power as the sole proprietor
of the Jigsaw legacy, Hoffman planned to run

00:15:59.760 --> 00:16:02.790
the city as he saw fit, and punish anyone
who got in his way.

00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:06.880
The only issue for him was that he was not
the last Jigsaw disciple left standing.

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:08.690
There was… another.

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:23.370
Shortly after the conclusion of the two simultaneous
games, Special Agent Strahm, who had been

00:16:23.370 --> 00:16:27.130
hot on the tail of Officer Rigg for most of
that day, showed up to Gideon Meat Packing

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:31.020
Plant with the hope that he could catch Jigsaw
or whichever disciple was responsible.

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:35.260
When Hoffman realized this, he saw the opportunity
to possibly take him out as well, and quietly

00:16:35.260 --> 00:16:39.350
closed the door, locking Strahm in the room
with the corpses of John, Amanda, Lynn Denlon

00:16:39.350 --> 00:16:41.541
and Jeff Denlon, before cutting the power.

00:16:41.541 --> 00:16:45.180
He then went to retrieve a pig mask to conceal
his identity in case of the possibility of

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:46.350
Strahm surviving.

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:49.640
John has left one more game behind for Agent
Strahm to find, and when he steps through

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:53.700
the secret door, which is his only path forward,
Hoffman takes him out and puts him to sleep.

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:56.410
While Strahm is out, he’s placed inside
the Water Cube trap…

00:16:56.410 --> 00:16:58.270
I guess Jigsaw watched too many Panic!

00:16:58.270 --> 00:17:01.940
At the Disco music videos on his deathbed
and got a little bit inspired.

00:17:01.940 --> 00:17:03.300
Maybe more than a little bit.

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:07.040
Hoffman assumes that Strahm is going to die
in the water cube and goes to rescue the Denlon’s

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:10.799
daughter, Corbett, who was being kept in a
cell as motivation for Jeff to win his game

00:17:10.799 --> 00:17:12.399
(which as we know, didn’t work out).

00:17:12.399 --> 00:17:15.679
So Hoffman brings her out to police where
he’s lauded as a hero for saving this girl

00:17:15.679 --> 00:17:19.530
from Jigsaw, but to his surprise, he and Corbett
are not the only two survivors.

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:23.129
Agent Strahm had punctured his trachea to
survive the Water Cube trap, and was being

00:17:23.129 --> 00:17:24.639
brought out to the ambulance.

00:17:24.639 --> 00:17:28.679
When Hoffman sees Strahm on a gurney, he knows
that his secret is not safe quite yet, not

00:17:28.679 --> 00:17:31.629
as long as Strahm and the FBI continue to
investigate.

00:17:31.629 --> 00:17:35.679
He makes up a story for the public about how
he too ended up falling into a Jigsaw trap,

00:17:35.679 --> 00:17:39.620
but his arm straps broke and he was able to
escape and find and rescue Corbett.

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:43.330
As the police continue to infiltrate Gideon
Meatpacking Plant, they eventually open the

00:17:43.330 --> 00:17:47.309
door and find the bodies of John, Amanda,
Lynn and Jeff, and send them to the morgue

00:17:47.309 --> 00:17:50.210
for an autopsy and this is where we have...
an error.

00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:51.210
Yeahhh…

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:54.419
I've been able to piece everything together
in a way that kind of makes sense up to this

00:17:54.419 --> 00:17:58.660
point, but I can't make any excuses for how
the dates kind of contradict each other here.

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:01.610
Remember how I said the simultaneous games
took place on April 28th?

00:18:01.610 --> 00:18:05.740
We know that because the fingerprints that
lead the FBI to Rigg came back on the 28th.

00:18:05.740 --> 00:18:10.500
The problem is that the date on John’s autopsy
tag is October 21st, 2006.

00:18:10.500 --> 00:18:11.929
See it clearly says it right there.

00:18:11.929 --> 00:18:13.929
I’m sure I’m not the first one to notice
this.

00:18:13.929 --> 00:18:15.240
What am I doing with my life?

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:19.460
It doesn’t take 6 months to do an autopsy,
and judging by the lack of decomposition on

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:20.460
John’s body…

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:22.690
I can’t show it but feel free to pull up
the movie if you don’t believe me… it

00:18:22.690 --> 00:18:23.690
hasn’t been very long.

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:27.539
So that means one of these dates has got to
be wrong, and all we can do is speculate on

00:18:27.539 --> 00:18:28.539
which one.

00:18:28.539 --> 00:18:30.470
And I think this one is wrong and John’s
death was in April.

00:18:30.470 --> 00:18:31.470
Here’s my rationale.

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:35.210
We later see Hoffman looking at evidence bags
containing Strahm’s cell phone and the pen

00:18:35.210 --> 00:18:36.779
he used to poke the hole in his neck.

00:18:36.779 --> 00:18:40.450
Those give us a month and day when the evidence
was collected, May 7th, and then the day that

00:18:40.450 --> 00:18:43.009
evidence was moved to storage, which appears
to be July 7th.

00:18:43.009 --> 00:18:46.841
It makes much more sense that they find the
evidence one week after Strahm escaped the

00:18:46.841 --> 00:18:48.760
crime scene, as opposed to 7 months after.

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:52.630
So let’s throw this tag out and say that
Jigsaw’s autopsy took place in late April

00:18:52.630 --> 00:18:55.870
or early May, and that’s when they found
the hidden tape in his stomach that has a

00:18:55.870 --> 00:18:57.480
message for Detective Hoffman.

00:18:57.480 --> 00:18:58.480
Roll it.

00:18:59.513 --> 00:19:02.357
JIGSAW: “Are you there Detective?

00:19:02.357 --> 00:19:07.149
If so, you are probably the last man standing.

00:19:07.149 --> 00:19:12.000
Now perhaps you will succeed where the others
have failed.

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:15.452
You think you will walk away untested?

00:19:15.452 --> 00:19:19.710
I promise that my work will continue.”

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:23.000
Clever wording by Jigsaw here to hide the
fact that he was working with Hoffman while

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:26.309
still delivering that message, since there
are other people in the room listening to

00:19:26.309 --> 00:19:27.309
the tape as well.

00:19:27.309 --> 00:19:31.460
In July, the police department holds a ceremony
honoring all of the fallen officers lost throughout

00:19:31.460 --> 00:19:36.059
the Saw saga, where Detective Hoffman is promoted
to Detective Lieutenant for his supposed heroics

00:19:36.059 --> 00:19:38.910
in putting an end to the Jigsaw murders and
saving Corbett.

00:19:38.910 --> 00:19:42.370
When receiving his service award he gives
a speech about how life must be cherished,

00:19:42.370 --> 00:19:44.279
and how true justice has been served.

00:19:44.279 --> 00:19:48.169
Again, this is also clever wording, because
the audience thinks he’s talking about John

00:19:48.169 --> 00:19:51.330
Kramer being brought to justice, when he’s
actually talking about everyone who went through

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:52.370
Kramer’s traps.

00:19:52.370 --> 00:19:55.690
And when he says life must be cherished, they
think he’s talking about the lives of the

00:19:55.690 --> 00:19:59.899
lost officers, but in reality he’s echoing
Jigsaw’s mantra, “Cherish your life.”

00:19:59.899 --> 00:20:04.230
By doing so, we learn one of the reasons that
Hoffman decides to continue operating as Jigsaw.

00:20:04.230 --> 00:20:07.840
It seems that he actually does believe in
John's values, he just has different standards

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:10.059
for judging whether or not people should be
punished.

00:20:10.059 --> 00:20:13.600
And like I said, and enjoy… continuing to
say because it's a very fun word to say…

00:20:13.600 --> 00:20:16.720
this is probably a result of his megalomania.

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:17.720
Megalomania.

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:21.370
Once the fallout of the end of the Jigsaw
case ended, Hoffman got to work on the next

00:20:21.370 --> 00:20:22.370
game.

00:20:22.370 --> 00:20:24.960
This is the one that John secretly told him
about when he was on his deathbed, and it

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:28.759
involves five people who all played a part
in causing a deadly fire, due to greed and

00:20:28.759 --> 00:20:29.919
corruption in their work.

00:20:29.919 --> 00:20:33.971
The specific traps and audio tapes were mostly
all created by Jigsaw before his death, so

00:20:33.971 --> 00:20:37.919
all Hoffman had to do was start and facilitate
the game, by keeping an eye on it using these

00:20:37.919 --> 00:20:38.919
computer monitors.

00:20:38.919 --> 00:20:42.370
He believed that this would be the last favor
he has to do for John Kramer.

00:20:42.370 --> 00:20:45.049
After that, he should have free reign to find
his own victims.

00:20:45.049 --> 00:20:48.419
But he hadn’t quite seized the full power
of Jigsaw just yet.

00:20:48.419 --> 00:20:51.669
Someone else was keeping an eye on him, and
they made it known by sending a note reading:

00:20:51.669 --> 00:20:55.200
“I know who you are,” the same note that
he received after killing Seth Baxter.

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:58.820
Remember, the first time he got this note,
it was from John himself, so Hoffman can only

00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:01.110
speculate on who might be sending this new
note.

00:21:01.110 --> 00:21:04.970
The only person Hoffman knows about who had
inside knowledge was Amanda, but she died

00:21:04.970 --> 00:21:06.260
that the same time as John.

00:21:06.260 --> 00:21:09.740
He knew Agent Strahm was onto him, but Strahm
wouldn't have known about the original note

00:21:09.740 --> 00:21:10.740
from 2004.

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:12.830
This new note had the exact same wording.

00:21:12.830 --> 00:21:16.720
His best guess is that it’s John's ex-wife
Jill Tuck, but it doesn't make sense for her

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:17.720
either.

00:21:17.720 --> 00:21:20.190
They had already seen each other at Gideon
Meat Packing Plant while they were putting

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:23.889
the final touches on the simultaneous games
back in April, so they both already knew about

00:21:23.889 --> 00:21:27.120
each other, and Hoffman didn’t have to worry
about her turning him in because she'd just

00:21:27.120 --> 00:21:29.190
be putting herself in danger by doing so.

00:21:29.190 --> 00:21:32.509
He’s called into the hospital where he finds
Agent Strahm sitting by Perez’s hospital

00:21:32.509 --> 00:21:33.063
bed.

00:21:33.063 --> 00:21:36.679
This leads him to believe the injuries she
had sustained in one of John’s traps had

00:21:36.679 --> 00:21:37.679
done her in.

00:21:37.679 --> 00:21:39.909
(She wasn’t really dead, I’m guessing
she was just moved to another room for more

00:21:39.909 --> 00:21:41.899
intensive care, but Hoffman didn’t know
that.)

00:21:41.899 --> 00:21:45.100
Strahm tells him that the last thing she said
was "Detective Hoffman".

00:21:45.100 --> 00:21:49.210
He finds this very suspicious, and… perhaps
unwisely… gives Hoffman an earful about

00:21:49.210 --> 00:21:52.987
how strange it is that Jigsaw would make a
mistake by letting Hoffman escape.

00:21:52.987 --> 00:21:54.222
HOFFMAN: “Jigsaw’s dead.”

00:21:54.222 --> 00:21:55.151
STRAHM: “I’m not talking about him.

00:21:55.151 --> 00:21:57.509
I’m talking about you and your whole crooked
department.”

00:21:57.509 --> 00:21:58.580
“My department’s gone.

00:21:58.580 --> 00:21:59.649
They’re all dead.

00:21:59.649 --> 00:22:01.080
There’s no one left.”

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:02.080
STRAHM: “Besides you.”

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:03.629
I feel so bad for Fisk.

00:22:03.629 --> 00:22:05.500
Nobody ever remembers Fisk….

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:06.807
Play the sad music.

00:22:06.807 --> 00:22:08.909
🎵 sad music 🎵

00:22:08.909 --> 00:22:12.700
After making sure he’s not being followed,
Hoffman visits the new underground lair where

00:22:12.700 --> 00:22:16.472
he’s monitoring the game, and gets there
just in time to see it begin.

00:22:23.490 --> 00:22:27.500
In Hoffman’s new lair, we see a miniature
model of the game that’s taking place, just

00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:29.779
like the models that John used to use for
his games.

00:22:29.779 --> 00:22:33.129
I think this is an important indicator of
how much Hoffman had learned from John in

00:22:33.129 --> 00:22:34.169
their years together.

00:22:34.169 --> 00:22:37.899
We previously saw John giving Hoffman pointers
on one of the traps, and now it would appear

00:22:37.899 --> 00:22:41.309
that Hoffman had learned everything about
his methods -- meaning that Hoffman could

00:22:41.309 --> 00:22:43.210
create his own games if he chose to.

00:22:43.210 --> 00:22:46.850
After checking in on the game, Hoffman decides
it’s best to contact Strahm and see if he

00:22:46.850 --> 00:22:48.830
can redirect his suspicions somewhere else.

00:22:48.830 --> 00:22:52.399
He calls the FBI’s headquarters from an
undisclosed location and asks for Strahm,

00:22:52.399 --> 00:22:55.430
but is instead connected to his superior,
Special Agent Erickson.

00:22:55.430 --> 00:22:58.600
Hoffman’s strategy is to use Strahm’s
own theory against him.

00:22:58.600 --> 00:23:02.480
He tells Erickson that Strahm believes there
was another accomplice outside of Amanda Young,

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:04.029
perhaps someone on the inside.

00:23:04.029 --> 00:23:07.120
He just doesn’t mention the part about it
possibly being him.

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:11.090
By doing this, he is secretly planting the
seed of Strahm being a suspect into Erickson’s

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:12.090
head.

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:15.299
It’s a risky move, because that’s sort
of a double edged sword, but it makes sense

00:23:15.299 --> 00:23:19.679
considering one of the side effects of megalomania
is cockiness and overconfidence.

00:23:19.679 --> 00:23:23.299
Erickson asks to reconnect with him the next
morning to discuss further, but Hoffman has

00:23:23.299 --> 00:23:27.460
one more idea to further the notion of Strahm
being a suspect in Erickson’s head.

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:31.500
He drives to where the FBI headquarters is
located and waits for Erickson to come out.

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:35.429
Then, using Strahm’s phone which he recovered
from the evidence bag, he calls Erickson and

00:23:35.429 --> 00:23:36.669
promptly hangs up.

00:23:36.669 --> 00:23:40.000
Erickson calls back, but he rejects the call
and turns off the device.

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:43.090
Another symptom of megalomania is manipulation
of others.

00:23:43.090 --> 00:23:46.509
Hoffman does this to remind Erickson about
the idea he planted in his head earlier, the

00:23:46.509 --> 00:23:48.389
idea that Strahm could be the second accomplice.

00:23:48.389 --> 00:23:52.399
It works, because Erickson tells his assistant
to start tracking Strahm’s phone.

00:23:52.399 --> 00:23:56.220
Now, Hoffman can lead them wherever he pleases
by simply leaving Strahm’s phone somewhere

00:23:56.220 --> 00:23:57.610
when he turns it back on.

00:23:57.610 --> 00:23:59.179
And that’s exactly what he does.

00:23:59.179 --> 00:24:02.919
He goes back to his lair, where the game is
being held, and leaves Strahm’s phone, along

00:24:02.919 --> 00:24:07.000
with several photos of Special Agent Erickson
to make it look like Strahm was Jigsaw’s

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:08.990
successor and his boss was the next target.

00:24:08.990 --> 00:24:12.909
To top it off, he leaves a hot beverage on
the desk to make it look like Strahm had just

00:24:12.909 --> 00:24:14.080
been there recently.

00:24:14.080 --> 00:24:17.519
From there, Hoffman went back to the Metropolitan
Police Department where Strahm was waiting

00:24:17.519 --> 00:24:18.630
for him in secret.

00:24:18.630 --> 00:24:22.690
He again shows what a skilled manipulator
he is, by looking suspicious as he left the

00:24:22.690 --> 00:24:24.769
building, thereby getting Strahm to follow
him.

00:24:24.769 --> 00:24:28.370
He led Strahm to an empty house where there
was another trap waiting in the basement.

00:24:28.370 --> 00:24:29.370
Let’s roll the tape.

00:24:30.637 --> 00:24:32.634
HOFFMAN: “Hello Agent Strahm.

00:24:32.634 --> 00:24:38.097
If you are hearing this then you have once
again found what you are looking for.

00:24:38.097 --> 00:24:39.285
Or so you think…

00:24:39.285 --> 00:24:43.965
As the old adage goes, fool me once, shame
on you...

00:24:43.965 --> 00:24:46.700
Fool me twice, shame on me.”

00:24:46.700 --> 00:24:51.280
He actually reveals how to escape from this
trap, but Strahm doesn’t trust him at all,

00:24:51.280 --> 00:24:52.280
and thus, doesn’t believe him.

00:24:52.280 --> 00:24:55.929
When Hoffman goes into the room, Strahm attacks
him and forces him into the box, not realizing

00:24:55.929 --> 00:24:59.809
that Hoffman had been telling the truth, and
the box was actually the only way to escape

00:24:59.809 --> 00:25:00.809
the room.

00:25:00.809 --> 00:25:04.540
The walls close in Star Wars garbage compactor
style and crush Agent Strahm.

00:25:04.540 --> 00:25:08.429
This is an excellent example of how Hoffman
had taken the teachings of Jigsaw and twisted

00:25:08.429 --> 00:25:09.990
them for his own benefit.

00:25:09.990 --> 00:25:14.029
His goal all along was to get rid of Strahm,
but he was unsuccessful in his first attempt.

00:25:14.029 --> 00:25:18.820
So the second time, he uses Jigsaw’s teachings,
specifically, his teachings about how to anticipate

00:25:18.820 --> 00:25:20.647
the human mind to his advantage.

00:25:22.079 --> 00:25:27.019
“If you’re good at anticipating the human
mind, it leaves nothing to chance.”

00:25:27.019 --> 00:25:30.440
After getting out of the glass box, Hoffman
recovers Strahm’s hand and uses it to plant

00:25:30.440 --> 00:25:33.679
fingerprints all over the crime scenes of
previous victims.

00:25:33.679 --> 00:25:37.279
Part of the genius of making everyone think
that Strahm was the second accomplice to Jigsaw

00:25:37.279 --> 00:25:41.169
was that Hoffman could continue to run new
traps and games, because everyone was out

00:25:41.169 --> 00:25:42.850
looking for Strahm and not him.

00:25:42.850 --> 00:25:46.440
The next trap that Hoffman would put into
action, however, would be another one designed

00:25:46.440 --> 00:25:47.539
by John Kramer.

00:25:47.539 --> 00:25:51.470
He had left instructions in his will for Jill
Tuck to carry out even more new traps.

00:25:51.470 --> 00:25:54.840
She most likely contacted Hoffman to get his
help with a few of them, because she’d never

00:25:54.840 --> 00:25:58.249
actually been involved in John’s operation
and didn’t have the experience.

00:25:58.249 --> 00:26:02.389
His next victims were a pair of malicious
lenders who repossess the valuables of desperate

00:26:02.389 --> 00:26:03.389
people in need.

00:26:03.389 --> 00:26:06.540
This was another game that was planned by
John, based on the fact that it’s his voice

00:26:06.540 --> 00:26:09.019
on the tape, but was never carried out before
his death.

00:26:09.019 --> 00:26:10.629
So Hoffman got to do it instead.

00:26:10.629 --> 00:26:13.830
The victims must see how it feels to give
up what is vital to them by sacrificing their

00:26:13.830 --> 00:26:16.509
own flesh into a scale in order to survive.

00:26:16.509 --> 00:26:20.030
After the conclusion of the test, Hoffman
does something interesting that Jigsaw had

00:26:20.030 --> 00:26:21.030
not.

00:26:21.030 --> 00:26:24.570
It seems he was recording audio of the victims
trying to escape rather than staying behind

00:26:24.570 --> 00:26:25.830
and witnessing it himself.

00:26:25.830 --> 00:26:29.499
He plays it back after the fact, either to
try to learn from the experience and improve

00:26:29.499 --> 00:26:33.630
his anticipation skills, or possibly just
for his own sick pleasure as he was starting

00:26:33.630 --> 00:26:35.370
to enjoy torturing people.

00:26:35.370 --> 00:26:38.850
After setting the trap, he’s called into
investigate it due to his unique position

00:26:38.850 --> 00:26:39.929
as Lieutenant Detective.

00:26:39.929 --> 00:26:43.700
There, he finds the FBI has taken over the
investigation and that they had discovered

00:26:43.700 --> 00:26:45.850
the Strahm fingerprints that he’d planted
there.

00:26:45.850 --> 00:26:49.700
He also discovers that Special Agent Perez,
who he previously thought to be deceased,

00:26:49.700 --> 00:26:52.440
was alive, well and working on this investigation.

00:26:52.440 --> 00:26:55.730
Erickson had led Hoffman to believe she was
dead earlier that month, because they actually

00:26:55.730 --> 00:26:59.869
had suspected Hoffman and wanted to have an
extra piece to play against him if need be.

00:26:59.869 --> 00:27:03.950
But now that Strahm was their #1 target, the
FBI offers to work with Hoffman.

00:27:03.950 --> 00:27:07.539
This is where Hoffman’s sociopathy really
shines more than ever before.

00:27:07.539 --> 00:27:09.769
He’s basically toying with the FBI at this
point.

00:27:09.769 --> 00:27:13.590
He feigns disinterest in working with them
because of their dishonesty about the fate

00:27:13.590 --> 00:27:18.350
of Ms. Perez, and this ultimately causes Erickson
to offer him full disclosure on all future

00:27:18.350 --> 00:27:20.139
secrets -- a deal that he accepts.

00:27:20.139 --> 00:27:23.426
“From now on, everything we know, you know.

00:27:23.426 --> 00:27:24.849
Is that fair?”

00:27:27.313 --> 00:27:32.679
The brilliant part is, he’s basically guaranteed
his own safety because he’s now working

00:27:32.679 --> 00:27:37.601
with both parties who are investigating Jigsaw:
The Metropolitan Police Department and the FBI.

00:27:37.601 --> 00:27:41.789
However, these two may have been the only
ones making a formal investigation, but there

00:27:41.789 --> 00:27:44.181
would be others in search of the truth.

00:27:50.110 --> 00:27:53.720
One day Hoffman goes to visit the survivor
of the flesh trap at the hospital, and on

00:27:53.720 --> 00:27:57.929
the way he runs into Pamela Jenkins, a journalist
that he accuses of twisting the facts to get

00:27:57.929 --> 00:27:58.950
a good story.

00:27:58.950 --> 00:28:00.789
Or as I like to call it, a journalist.

00:28:00.789 --> 00:28:04.559
Jenkins promises to dial down her articles
if Mark can connect her with Jill Tuck, so

00:28:04.559 --> 00:28:05.940
he agrees to help her out.

00:28:05.940 --> 00:28:09.249
My guess is that he did so because he felt
he would have even more power if he had the

00:28:09.249 --> 00:28:10.600
media on his side too.

00:28:10.600 --> 00:28:14.340
From there, he goes to visit the other victim
of the flesh trap, the one who didn’t make it. 

00:28:14.340 --> 00:28:18.460
The autopsy reveals a different knife was
used to cut the Jigsaw piece compared to all

00:28:18.460 --> 00:28:23.240
the other victims besides one, and that one
is Seth Baxter, the man who killed Hoffman’s sister. 

00:28:23.240 --> 00:28:26.940
The FBI wants to find the Seth Baxter tape,
and if Strahm’s voice is on it, that will

00:28:26.940 --> 00:28:29.340
be all the evidence they need to publicly
convict him.

00:28:29.340 --> 00:28:33.049
But I think they also secretly did still suspect
Hoffman a little bit, because he obviously

00:28:33.049 --> 00:28:34.360
did have the motive.

00:28:34.360 --> 00:28:37.910
That night, he goes to visit Jill Tuck at
her work, and demands she give up the contents

00:28:37.910 --> 00:28:39.679
of the box left to her in John’s will.

00:28:39.679 --> 00:28:42.210
This is just a prime example of Hoffman’s
megalomania.

00:28:42.210 --> 00:28:46.799
He is so desperate to control everything and
everyone that he’s going to go against John’s

00:28:46.799 --> 00:28:50.899
dying wishes and usurp power from one of the
few people that knows about him so that he

00:28:50.899 --> 00:28:53.309
can control all aspects of the game.

00:28:53.309 --> 00:28:56.950
He makes her hand over the files of the victims
who are to be tested next, a group of people

00:28:56.950 --> 00:29:01.159
who all work for, or are connected to this
dirty insurance company called Umbrella Health.

00:29:01.159 --> 00:29:05.179
Meanwhile, Erickson and Perez are continuing
the re-investigation of the Seth Baxter case,

00:29:05.179 --> 00:29:06.980
and ask Hoffman to come in to see their findings.

00:29:06.980 --> 00:29:10.539
They’re able to uncover the master tape
left behind at the pendulum trap, but it’s

00:29:10.539 --> 00:29:14.250
been intentionally distorted, so they send
it to a lab to be descrambled.

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:18.160
This makes Hoffman nervous because he knows
that’s his voice on the tape, and they will

00:29:18.160 --> 00:29:21.380
eventually uncover it but he has no choice
but to travel with them to the lab.

00:29:21.380 --> 00:29:24.090
SP. AGENT ERICKSON: “Come on, you’re coming right?”

00:29:24.090 --> 00:29:25.179
“Of course.”

00:29:25.179 --> 00:29:28.179
It can be assumed that they go through some
kind of security checkpoint before entering

00:29:28.179 --> 00:29:30.809
the FBI facility to separate Hoffman from
his gun.

00:29:30.809 --> 00:29:33.639
Like I said, they were secretly a bit suspicious
of him still.

00:29:33.639 --> 00:29:38.269
The scene at the lab where the audio is defiltered
is perhaps my favorite scene in the Saw franchise.

00:29:38.269 --> 00:29:42.340
As the technician gets closer and closer to
uncovering Hoffman’s real voice, the tension

00:29:42.340 --> 00:29:43.340
in the room builds.

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:45.466
SP. AGENT PEREZ: “You OK?”

00:29:45.466 --> 00:29:46.466
HOFFMAN: “Huh?”

00:29:46.466 --> 00:29:47.910
“You seem a little preoccupied.”

00:29:47.910 --> 00:29:50.320
“Oh, I’m just anxious about the tape.”

00:29:50.320 --> 00:29:54.529
The pressure in Hoffman’s chest continues
to rise when Perez expresses her doubt that

00:29:54.529 --> 00:29:58.110
Strahm is really responsible, based on his
past behaviour as her partner.

00:29:58.110 --> 00:30:01.429
She’s basically making it clear to Hoffman
that she never believed he was innocent.

00:30:01.429 --> 00:30:06.020
And you can just see Hoffman struggling to
keep his cool as his big lie slowly unfolds

00:30:06.020 --> 00:30:07.460
in front of these FBI agents.

00:30:07.460 --> 00:30:11.130
He’s quickly trying to throw together a
plan in his head, using only a few items from

00:30:11.130 --> 00:30:13.960
a snack table to prepare for the moment when
he’s exposed.

00:30:13.960 --> 00:30:17.917
But the big bomb is the moment when Erickson
drops another discovery.

00:30:17.917 --> 00:30:18.639
Roll.

00:30:18.639 --> 00:30:19.139
That.

00:30:19.139 --> 00:30:20.139
Clip.

00:30:21.460 --> 00:30:27.769
SP AGENT ERICKSON: “In other words, when he left his fingerprints
on the latest victims, Strahm was already

00:30:27.769 --> 00:30:28.317
dead!”

00:30:28.317 --> 00:30:30.317
TAPE: “Right now you’re feeling helpless.”
LAB TECH: “There it is!”

00:30:30.317 --> 00:30:35.669
After two years of quietly helping Jigsaw,
and after months of effort diverting the Metropolitan

00:30:35.669 --> 00:30:40.619
Police Department and the FBI, Mark Hoffman
was finally backed into a corner, but he wasn’t

00:30:40.619 --> 00:30:42.490
about to go down without a fight.

00:30:42.490 --> 00:30:46.119
He slashes Erickson with a pastry knife and
burns Perez with a piping hot coffee.

00:30:46.119 --> 00:30:50.350
He uses the lab technician as a meat shield,
protecting himself from Perez’s gunshots

00:30:50.350 --> 00:30:52.860
long enough to get to her and take her out
as well.

00:30:52.860 --> 00:30:56.166
Before finishing her off, he asks who else
knows about him, to which she replies…

00:30:56.166 --> 00:30:57.602
“EVERYONE.”

00:30:57.603 --> 00:30:59.603
"Everyone?"
"EVERYONE!!!"

00:30:59.879 --> 00:31:04.080
With that, he retrieves a can of gasolene
from his car and comes back to burn the whole

00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:05.919
******** place to the ground.

00:31:05.919 --> 00:31:09.830
One of the most brutal details in this scene
highlights how much of a psychopath Hoffman

00:31:09.830 --> 00:31:10.830
had become.

00:31:10.830 --> 00:31:14.779
When he returns to the lab, he notices Erickson
still alive, and rather than put him out of

00:31:14.779 --> 00:31:19.019
his suffering before starting the fire, he
chooses the most painful punishment imaginable

00:31:19.019 --> 00:31:23.500
and covers him in even more gasolene before
lighting a flame and tossing the match.

00:31:23.500 --> 00:31:27.289
With his pursuers now taken care of, he returns
to the zoo, the site of the game that was

00:31:27.289 --> 00:31:29.809
currently taking place, just in time to see
the end of it.

00:31:29.809 --> 00:31:34.090
But when he gets there, he finds a relic from
his past, which fills him with even more uncertainty.

00:31:34.090 --> 00:31:38.909
It’s the same note he left for Amanda three
months ago, threatening her to kill Lynn Denlon.

00:31:38.909 --> 00:31:42.910
He realizes it’s nearly impossible for anyone
to know about this, and knows he’s still

00:31:42.910 --> 00:31:44.100
in trouble when he sees it.

00:31:44.100 --> 00:31:47.309
When he sits down in his chair, he’s jolted
by a powerful wave of electricity.

00:31:47.309 --> 00:31:49.869
Whoever had been there had booby-trapped the
seat.

00:31:49.869 --> 00:31:52.950
When he comes to, he discovers who was responsible:
Jill Tuck.

00:31:52.950 --> 00:31:56.909
She had hidden one thing left for her in her
ex-husband’s will: the fact that Mark Hoffman

00:31:56.909 --> 00:31:59.880
was also meant to endure one more test.

00:31:59.880 --> 00:32:03.040
Hoffman knew this after hearing the tape found
in Kramer’s stomach at the autopsy, but

00:32:03.040 --> 00:32:05.399
after three months he thought he was in the
clear.

00:32:05.399 --> 00:32:09.840
Jill subjects him to a classic Jigsaw device,
the reverse bear trap, and leaves him in the

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:11.175
room to die.

00:32:17.352 --> 00:32:21.359
Jill’s instructions were to leave Hoffman
restrained in the reverse bear trap as punishment

00:32:21.359 --> 00:32:23.610
for deviating from Jigsaw’s plan.

00:32:23.610 --> 00:32:24.980
Hoffman was supposed to die there.

00:32:24.980 --> 00:32:27.940
The only problem was that Jill Tuck was no
Jigsaw.

00:32:27.940 --> 00:32:32.200
She had not thought out every possible scenario
the way John might have, and Hoffman is able

00:32:32.200 --> 00:32:35.759
to exploit this by breaking his hand with
the machine fastened to his face, using it

00:32:35.759 --> 00:32:39.630
to untie himself from the chair and lodging
the reverse bear trap into the steel bars

00:32:39.630 --> 00:32:41.330
covering the window on the door.

00:32:41.330 --> 00:32:44.980
This prevents it from tearing his face open
when time expires, and gives him just enough

00:32:44.980 --> 00:32:46.051
time to escape from it’s confines.

00:32:46.051 --> 00:32:51.230
His jaw is damaged in the incident, but he
does survive, and swears to have his revenge

00:32:51.230 --> 00:32:52.230
on Jill.

00:32:52.230 --> 00:32:55.529
Aftering taping up his injured hand, he takes
the reverse bear trap with him and retreats

00:32:55.529 --> 00:32:57.970
to a new safehouse at an abandoned air hangar.

00:32:57.970 --> 00:32:59.590
He fixes himself up.

00:32:59.590 --> 00:33:03.070
Realizing that Jill got away and will probably
go to the police, he knows he’ll never be

00:33:03.070 --> 00:33:06.700
able to go back to his old life and destroys
his cell phone and ID cards.

00:33:06.700 --> 00:33:10.610
He also decides to lie low for a while, and
goes into hiding for the next 8 months, not

00:33:10.610 --> 00:33:12.700
emerging again until March of 2007.

00:33:12.700 --> 00:33:16.600
During the time in hiding, he used his inside
knowledge of the police headquarters to hack

00:33:16.600 --> 00:33:19.100
into the station’s security system and spy
on them.

00:33:19.100 --> 00:33:23.059
With him out, and most of his department dead,
the police chief brings back an old face to

00:33:23.059 --> 00:33:25.104
take over the Jigsaw investigation:

00:33:25.104 --> 00:33:28.631
🎵 EPIC MUSIC 🎵

00:33:28.631 --> 00:33:31.274
sorry Fisk… he went with Officer Matt Gibson.

00:33:31.274 --> 00:33:33.751
🎵 sad music 🎵
FISK: "Jeez."

00:33:33.751 --> 00:33:38.188
By the way, remember way, way back about a half hour ago when I told you he might come back?

00:33:38.188 --> 00:33:42.951
“Gibson transferred to Internal Affairs,
where he busted three of Hoffman’s men, 

00:33:42.951 --> 00:33:45.023
and Hoffman swore he’d have his revenge.

00:33:45.023 --> 00:33:46.634
Just remember that, because it could come
back.

00:33:46.634 --> 00:33:48.174
I’m not saying it’s gonna come back.

00:33:48.174 --> 00:33:50.655
But it might come back.”

00:33:50.655 --> 00:33:53.720
Ahh yes, good memories of when I was a younger
creator.

00:33:53.720 --> 00:33:56.580
So Hoffman kept his eye on whatever was going
on at the police station.

00:33:56.580 --> 00:34:00.279
He also had information on three more groups
that Jigsaw never had time to test.

00:34:00.279 --> 00:34:02.330
So his next objective is to carry these out.

00:34:02.330 --> 00:34:05.779
First, he captures three participants of a
toxic love triangle and puts them through

00:34:05.779 --> 00:34:07.739
a unique trap that takes place in public.

00:34:07.739 --> 00:34:10.690
The two men have to execute their former lover
to survive.

00:34:10.690 --> 00:34:14.440
Next, four racists are trapped in an elaborate
setup at Pete’s Auto Body Shop and there

00:34:14.440 --> 00:34:15.440
are no survivors.

00:34:15.440 --> 00:34:18.850
He leaves a note for Gibson on the bathroom
mirror, along with the reverse bear trap which

00:34:18.850 --> 00:34:20.680
is covered in Jill Tuck’s fingerprints.

00:34:20.680 --> 00:34:24.179
As if that’s a reliable measure of anything
at this point, considering all we’ve been

00:34:24.179 --> 00:34:25.179
through.

00:34:25.179 --> 00:34:28.970
And finally, the last game of the Jigsaw saga
is held on March 12th and tests a man named

00:34:28.970 --> 00:34:32.929
Bobby Dagen, who had falsely claimed to be
a survivor of a Jigsaw trap in order to sell

00:34:32.929 --> 00:34:33.929
his book.

00:34:33.929 --> 00:34:36.950
Hopefully I’m not the only one who appreciates
the irony of Hoffman, who once pretended to

00:34:36.950 --> 00:34:40.810
be Jigsaw for personal gain, now setting up
a real trap for Bobby, who had pretended to

00:34:40.810 --> 00:34:42.380
be a victim for personal gain.

00:34:42.380 --> 00:34:45.810
With the last of these traps set into motion,
Hoffman could focus all of his efforts on

00:34:45.810 --> 00:34:49.850
what now mattered most to him, getting revenge
on Jill Tuck and Matt Gibson.

00:34:49.850 --> 00:34:53.850
He starts by sending a DVD to Gibson’s safehouse,
in which he openly shows his face and requests

00:34:53.850 --> 00:34:55.360
that Gibson hands over Jill Tuck.

00:34:55.360 --> 00:34:59.070
If he complies, Hoffman says he will stop
the last game, and the killing will end.

00:34:59.070 --> 00:35:02.770
But Gibson has promised immunity to Jill and
isn’t about to trust a bargain made by a

00:35:02.770 --> 00:35:03.770
serial killer.

00:35:03.770 --> 00:35:07.290
Hoffman’s next communication with Gibson
is an email with a video message, giving him

00:35:07.290 --> 00:35:11.090
a cryptic clue, that he must look beyond the
crossroad to the clear dawn.

00:35:11.090 --> 00:35:14.760
Look beyond the crossroad is a reference to
Crossroads Manufacturing, which is the abandoned

00:35:14.760 --> 00:35:18.560
factory turned flophouse where Hoffman saved
Gibson’s life all those years ago, and clear

00:35:18.560 --> 00:35:22.160
dawn is a reference to Clear Dawn Psychiatric
Hospital, where the man who nearly killed

00:35:22.160 --> 00:35:23.460
Gibson used to be a patient.

00:35:23.460 --> 00:35:26.921
That’s where the Bobby Dagen game is being
held, but Hoffman goes back to Pete’s Auto

00:35:26.921 --> 00:35:30.990
Body, which is where the Car Trap was located
and remotely detonates a bomb nearby in order

00:35:30.990 --> 00:35:35.010
to lure all of the cops, who were still collecting
evidence, outside for a little while.

00:35:35.010 --> 00:35:38.580
Hoffman uses this time to go into a hidden
room inside the auto body shop and check on

00:35:38.580 --> 00:35:40.880
the video feed of the game using the monitors.

00:35:40.880 --> 00:35:44.070
Then he sets up a trap for Gibson and his
men, who would soon be on their way after

00:35:44.070 --> 00:35:46.110
they trace his email back to this room.

00:35:46.110 --> 00:35:50.370
And this is the moment that all of the training
with Jigsaw finally pays off for Hoffman.

00:35:50.370 --> 00:35:54.440
He had studied under John Kramer to learn
the ways of a great mechanical engineer, he

00:35:54.440 --> 00:35:58.480
had practiced for nearly 3 years by tweaking
and carrying out John’s traps and he had

00:35:58.480 --> 00:36:01.970
obtained the experience needed to fully create
a trap of his own.

00:36:01.970 --> 00:36:06.450
He disregarded John’s code of conduct though
-- this trap’s intention was not to rehabilitate,

00:36:06.450 --> 00:36:07.450
but rather, to kill.

00:36:07.450 --> 00:36:11.060
He removed the body of one of the victims
of the Car Trap and placed him in the room

00:36:11.060 --> 00:36:14.760
wearing Jigsaw’s cloak, so that Gibson would
be lured into the trap and executed by the

00:36:14.760 --> 00:36:17.480
motion activated turret hidden in the corner
of the room.

00:36:17.480 --> 00:36:20.020
We know that Hoffman had long desired his
revenge on Gibson,

00:36:20.020 --> 00:36:21.080
but the fact that he wasn’t

00:36:21.080 --> 00:36:24.410
even there for his death shows where Mark’s
priorities were at the time.

00:36:24.410 --> 00:36:27.740
He was still mad about Jill Tuck trying to
kill him off in the reverse bear trap and

00:36:27.740 --> 00:36:31.960
then selling him out to the cops, and his
one burning desire at that point was to get

00:36:31.960 --> 00:36:33.620
to her and have his revenge.

00:36:33.620 --> 00:36:37.390
He hid himself in the now empty body bag of
the fourth victim, which was a clever way

00:36:37.390 --> 00:36:39.480
of sneaking himself into the police station.

00:36:39.480 --> 00:36:41.520
Once there, he went on a huge killing spree.

00:36:41.520 --> 00:36:42.810
It started with the coroner.

00:36:42.810 --> 00:36:46.860
This was the man whose discovery about the
knife that cut each Jigsaw piece led the FBI

00:36:46.860 --> 00:36:51.170
to reopen their investigation about Seth Baxter,
which ultimately led to Hoffman getting caught.

00:36:51.170 --> 00:36:55.340
Now I may need Dead Meat to back me up on
this, I’m the History expert, not the Math

00:36:55.340 --> 00:36:58.730
expert, but according to my calculations,
I believe he kills five more people at the

00:36:58.730 --> 00:37:02.080
police station on his way to the holding cell
where Jill is being kept.

00:37:02.080 --> 00:37:05.590
When he gets into the cell, he forcefully
grabs her by the hair and asks, “how do

00:37:05.590 --> 00:37:07.970
I look,” which seems like a weird thing
to say at first…

00:37:07.970 --> 00:37:11.440
I mean they’re not going on a date, they’re
basically trying to kill each other, which

00:37:11.440 --> 00:37:14.850
is usually more of a 3rd date activity for
me, but I think the line is a reference to

00:37:14.850 --> 00:37:17.930
the fact that this is the first time they’ve
seen each other since Jill put the reverse

00:37:17.930 --> 00:37:21.370
bear trap on him, which messed up his jaw
and permanently changed his appearance.

00:37:21.370 --> 00:37:24.980
She stabs him in the neck with a nail file
and scampers away, where she hides in the

00:37:24.980 --> 00:37:25.980
evidence room.

00:37:25.980 --> 00:37:29.890
But Hoffman, who was again able to use his
Jigsaw powers to anticipate her actions, goes

00:37:29.890 --> 00:37:33.601
in after her, but Jill Tuck would not be the
only thing found there.

00:37:39.600 --> 00:37:43.370
After discovering Jill’s hiding place, Hoffman
realized exactly where he was.

00:37:43.370 --> 00:37:46.690
He was in the evidence room, meaning that
he would have access to the very same tool

00:37:46.690 --> 00:37:50.478
that Jill had tried to kill him with 8 months
ago, the reverse bear trap.

00:37:50.478 --> 00:37:55.430
This is actually the version of the Bear Trap that Jigsaw had
 used to test Amanda way back when she was being tested.

00:37:55.430 --> 00:37:59.530
He finds it most fitting to leave Jill behind
in the same position on the same chair she

00:37:59.530 --> 00:38:03.490
had shocked him with the previous summer,
but unlike Hoffman, Jill does not have what

00:38:03.490 --> 00:38:05.570
it takes to escape, and she is killed.

00:38:05.570 --> 00:38:08.980
After the trap ends her life, Hoffman closes
with Jigsaw’s iconic phrase…

00:38:08.980 --> 00:38:10.510
“Game over.”

00:38:10.510 --> 00:38:14.550
Even a small change like this, saying Game
Over after the victim’s death as opposed

00:38:14.550 --> 00:38:17.880
to before, is indicative of how Hoffman’s
version of Jigsaw refused to play by the rules

00:38:17.880 --> 00:38:19.850
the way that Kramer’s version of Jigsaw
did.

00:38:19.850 --> 00:38:24.460
Hoffman has his own dirty way of doing things,
that is inspired by Jigsaw, but not limited

00:38:24.460 --> 00:38:25.990
by a code of conduct.

00:38:25.990 --> 00:38:29.710
Having gotten his revenge, he retires to his
secret safehouse in the hangar where he prepares

00:38:29.710 --> 00:38:31.600
a bag full of money and supplies.

00:38:31.600 --> 00:38:34.630
He’s probably planning to go into hiding
again for a while, since he just took out

00:38:34.630 --> 00:38:38.070
an entire police station which is likely to
draw more federal attention.

00:38:38.070 --> 00:38:41.120
Wanting to clear out everything and start
somewhere new, he lights up the hangar and

00:38:41.120 --> 00:38:44.970
on his way out, but before he can make it
very far he’s captured, by three men in

00:38:44.970 --> 00:38:46.180
pig masks.

00:38:46.180 --> 00:38:49.680
Throughout the course of this saga, Hoffman
had brilliantly accounted for everything his

00:38:49.680 --> 00:38:53.810
opposition would throw at him, but there was
still one thing he was not able to solve:

00:38:53.810 --> 00:38:56.310
who had sent him the note: “I know who you
are.”

00:38:56.310 --> 00:39:00.741
The answer would take Hoffman back to 2004,
where one of his first tasks under John Kramer

00:39:00.741 --> 00:39:04.640
was to plant a doctor’s penlight at one
of his crime scenes, and that doctor was named

00:39:04.640 --> 00:39:05.900
Lawrence Gordon.

00:39:05.900 --> 00:39:09.830
Unbeknownst to Hoffman, Gordon had become
a secret apprentice to Jigsaw after surviving

00:39:09.830 --> 00:39:14.180
the bathroom game in 2004 and was working
behind the scenes for John ever since.

00:39:14.180 --> 00:39:18.220
He kept an eye on Hoffman after John’s death
and was instructed to act immediately if anything

00:39:18.220 --> 00:39:19.710
were to happen to Jill.

00:39:19.710 --> 00:39:22.930
According to John’s orders, Gordon was to
take Hoffman to the same bathroom where he

00:39:22.930 --> 00:39:25.560
had once been tested, and leave him with no
escape.

00:39:25.560 --> 00:39:27.210
Hoffman desperately reaches for the hacksaw,
knowing that his best chance to escape was

00:39:27.210 --> 00:39:29.230
to sever his own foot, but Gordon takes it
and throws it out of reach.

00:39:29.230 --> 00:39:31.310
“Game over.”

00:39:33.140 --> 00:39:36.370
Notice how he does it correctly, by saying
it before the victim’s death.

00:39:36.370 --> 00:39:37.370
Just saying.

00:39:37.370 --> 00:39:40.540
So we’re here, we’re almost at the end
of this very long video, but we’ve actually

00:39:40.540 --> 00:39:45.100
hit a bit of a point of contention among Saw
fans, because that’s the last we see of

00:39:45.100 --> 00:39:48.170
Hoffman, and there’s a bit of divide over
his ultimate fate.

00:39:48.170 --> 00:39:49.750
Did he die in the bathroom?

00:39:49.750 --> 00:39:51.720
Or would he have found some way to escape?

00:39:51.720 --> 00:39:54.420
There are even conflicting reports from the
creators of the film.

00:39:54.420 --> 00:39:58.350
In an audio commentary on Saw 7, they mention
that Hoffman is dead, but according to the

00:39:58.350 --> 00:40:02.990
actor that played him, Costas Mandylor, or
as I like to call him, The Mandylorian, Hoffman

00:40:02.990 --> 00:40:04.160
is still alive.

00:40:04.160 --> 00:40:06.780
So with that all being said, I have the answer.

00:40:06.780 --> 00:40:07.780
Hoffman’s dead.

00:40:07.780 --> 00:40:08.930
And here’s why.

00:40:08.930 --> 00:40:12.700
Outside of the fact that it would totally
undo Saws 4-7 and make them essentially pointless,

00:40:12.700 --> 00:40:14.290
let’s look at the canon for our answer.

00:40:14.290 --> 00:40:17.470
We don’t see anything that happened after
Hoffman was left in the bathroom until the

00:40:17.470 --> 00:40:20.350
next sequel, Jigsaw, came out in 2017.

00:40:20.350 --> 00:40:24.540
In Jigsaw, the killings begin again after
10 years of inactivity, and another investigation

00:40:24.540 --> 00:40:25.540
opens up.

00:40:25.540 --> 00:40:29.430
Nobody, at any point, mentions Hoffman as
a possible suspect, and this is most likely

00:40:29.430 --> 00:40:32.470
because they eventually discovered the bathroom
and found him dead in there.

00:40:32.470 --> 00:40:36.010
And yes, we do know that they discovered the
bathroom because Eleanor has a replica of

00:40:36.010 --> 00:40:39.700
the hacksaws, and these weren’t something
that could be found in any other location.

00:40:39.700 --> 00:40:43.230
So that’s why I think Hoffman is almost
definitely dead, and I say almost because

00:40:43.230 --> 00:40:46.330
it could be that he escaped and he’s in
jail somewhere, but if that was the case,

00:40:46.330 --> 00:40:49.540
you’d think the new investigators in Jigsaw
would at least double check that he hadn’t

00:40:49.540 --> 00:40:50.540
escaped or something.

00:40:50.540 --> 00:40:53.640
And on top of that, the end of his story is
just a more fitting way to go out.

00:40:53.640 --> 00:40:57.421
But until we see his grave or some kind of
confirmation in a future sequel, I know that

00:40:57.421 --> 00:41:00.940
you Hoffman fans aren’t ever gonna shut
up, so I’ll just leave at that for now.

00:41:00.940 --> 00:41:03.900
Let me know your opinion on that in the comments
though and if you want to see some more of

00:41:03.900 --> 00:41:07.360
your favorite Saw characters on Horror History
then click that playlist on the left.

00:41:07.360 --> 00:41:11.610
And if you love horror, if you love Saw, there’s
gonna be more content on the way soon.

00:41:11.610 --> 00:41:15.580
We’re getting close to a new Saw movie coming
out so make sure you subscribe to CZsWorld

00:41:15.580 --> 00:41:19.990
for new horrors every week, RING that deathbell
for notifications and I’ll see you in the

00:41:19.990 --> 00:41:20.990
next one.

00:41:20.990 --> 00:41:24.590
Megalomania, megalomania, megalomania, assuming
we both survive.

